<article>
    <h1>{$selectedSection}</h1>
    <section>
        <p>
            The physical quantities {`\\(\\vec{r}, \\vec{v}\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{a}\\)`} depend on the frame of reference. Some times working in a different frame makes analysis simpler. Consider the motion of an object seen from two frames of reference {`\\(O\\)`} and {`\\(O'\\)`}. It is possible that {`\\(O'\\)`} is moving with respect to {`\\(O\\)`}. We'll write {`\\(\\vec{r}_{OA}\\)`} to represent the position at {`\\(A\\)`} with respect to frame with origin at {`\\(O\\)`}. The key idea for the <em>change of frame</em> is the triangle law of vectior addition.    
