        <p>
            Consider the diagram above. The position of the object at {`\\(P\\)`} is {`\\(\\vec{r}_{OP}\\)`} with respect to frame {`\\(O\\)`} and {`\\(\\vec{r}_{O'P}\\)`} with respect to frame {`\\(O'\\)`}. Suppose the position of {`\\(O'\\)`} with respect to {`\\(O\\)`} is written as {`\\(\\vec{r}_{OO'}\\)`}. Applying the law of vector addition in the triangle {`\\(\\Delta OO'P\\)`}, we get {`\\[\\vec{r}_{OP} = \\vec{r}_{OO'} + \\vec{r}_{O'P}\\]`}
            <em>Position with respect to {`\\(O\\)`} is equal to position of {`\\(O'\\)`} plus position with respect to {`\\(O'\\)`}. </em>
        </p>
        <p>Using this relation, it becomes trivial to relate velocities and accelerations. {
            `\\begin{eqnarray}
                \\frac{d}{dt}\\vec{r}_{OP} &=& \\frac{d}{dt}\\vec{r}_{OO'} + \\frac{d}{dt}\\vec{r}_{O'P}\\\\
                \\implies\\vec{v}_{OP} &=& \\vec{v}_{OO'} + \\vec{v}_{O'P}\\\\
                \\frac{d}{dt}\\vec{v}_{OP} &=& \\frac{d}{dt}\\vec{v}_{OO'} + \\frac{d}{dt}\\vec{v}_{O'P}\\\\
                \\implies\\vec{a}_{OP} &=& \\vec{a}_{OO'} + \\vec{a}_{O'P}
            \\end{eqnarray}
            `}
            </p>
